WEBVTT

NOTE Internet Monitor tour: report (en). Generated by tools/tutorial/build.cjs.

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.187
The Monthly report puts a
whole month on one page.

2
00:00:03.187 --> 00:00:07.057
It's made to be printed,
or sent to your provider with a complaint.

3
00:00:07.057 --> 00:00:09.367
Open it from History or from Complaints.

4
00:00:09.767 --> 00:00:13.300
Use the arrows to move to
an earlier month, and back.

5
00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:17.115
At the top are your provider,
plan, line number, and how this

6
00:00:17.115 --> 00:00:20.978
computer was connected, from the
line details on the Complaints page.

7
00:00:20.978 --> 00:00:23.833
A cable to the router makes
the strongest evidence.

8
00:00:24.233 --> 00:00:29.010
The tiles sum up the month: uptime,
the number of outages, total downtime,

9
00:00:29.010 --> 00:00:33.465
the longest outage, how long recovery
took on average, unstable time,

10
00:00:33.465 --> 00:00:38.500
and monitoring coverage, which is the
share of the month the app was watching.

11
00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:44.500
Downtime by day shows which days were bad.
Point at a bar to see that day's numbers.

12
00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:50.367
What failed shows which checks failed during
the outages, and their share of the downtime.

13
00:00:50.767 --> 00:00:54.749
The outages table lists each outage
with its day, time and length.

14
00:00:54.749 --> 00:00:58.550
Under the cause, the app adds
what it learned from your router,

15
00:00:58.550 --> 00:01:01.567
and from a route test it
runs when the line drops.

16
00:01:01.967 --> 00:01:04.706
For example: the line to
the provider was down,

17
00:01:04.706 --> 00:01:08.378
or the line was up but the provider
ended the internet session.

18
00:01:08.378 --> 00:01:11.700
Both point at the provider,
and they are strong evidence.

19
00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:15.500
Downtime by hour of day shows
whether outages cluster at

20
00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:18.900
certain hours, which is a sign
of an overloaded network.

21
00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:22.876
Speed tests versus your plan
compares your tests with the

22
00:01:22.876 --> 00:01:26.767
speed you pay for, including
evenings against the other hours.

23
00:01:27.167 --> 00:01:30.135
What the downtime is worth
estimates the value of

24
00:01:30.135 --> 00:01:33.224
the time you were offline,
from your monthly price.

25
00:01:33.224 --> 00:01:37.767
Use it to ask for a refund.
Outages that look like power cuts are left out.

26
00:01:38.167 --> 00:01:42.292
Not monitored lists the times when
this computer was off or asleep.

27
00:01:42.292 --> 00:01:45.247
They are never counted as
outages, so the report

28
00:01:45.247 --> 00:01:47.833
stays fair, and the provider can trust it.

29
00:01:48.233 --> 00:01:51.325
Complaints filed lists your
complaints for the month.

30
00:01:51.325 --> 00:01:56.167
Log integrity shows that the saved logs
were not changed after each day was sealed.

31
00:01:56.567 --> 00:02:00.367
Print or save as PDF opens
your browser's print window.

32
00:02:00.367 --> 00:02:04.652
To get a file you can send,
choose Save as PDF as the printer.

33
00:02:04.652 --> 00:02:07.900
Buttons and menus are
left out of the printout.

34
00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:12.087
Download evidence pack saves
a ZIP with this report,

35
00:02:12.087 --> 00:02:15.582
all the month's raw logs,
and SHA-256 checksums.

36
00:02:15.582 --> 00:02:17.767
Send it together with the PDF.

37
00:02:18.167 --> 00:02:23.233
Press How this page works on any page to
watch its video. Thanks for watching!
